<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wang Xizhi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">303–<a href="/years/361">361</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-clan-of-langya">Wang clan of Langya</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wang Xizhi was a Chinese calligrapher of the Eastern Jin dynasty, revered as the 'Sage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">Calligraphy</a>'. A member of the powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-clan-of-langya">Wang clan of Langya</a>, he served as an official in Kuaiji. His preface to the poems composed at the Orchid Pavilion gathering of 353 became the most celebrated work of Chinese calligraphy; no originals survive, but copies and rubbings set the standard for running and regular script for centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wang-xizhi.jpg" alt="Wang Xizhi" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown Chinese artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_Wang_Xizhi_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wang Xizhi was born around 303 in Linyi, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langya-commandery">Langya Commandery</a>, into the aristocratic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-clan-of-langya">Wang clan of Langya</a>. His father, Wang Kuang, was governor of Huainan. After the Western Jin collapsed the family fled to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and helped establish the Eastern Jin in 317. He studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a> under Wei Shuo, his father's cousin, and under his uncle Wang Yi, and after his parents died he lived in the household of his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-dao">Wang Dao</a>. Around 323 he became engaged to Xi Xuan, daughter of the general Xi Jian. They had eight recorded children, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-xianzhi">Wang Xianzhi</a>, who also became a skilled calligrapher; the two were later called the Two Wangs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Between 324 and 354 Wang held various government posts, beginning as an assistant in the Palace Library. He served as a military aide to the general Yu Liang, became governor of Linchuan in 336, and was administrator of Kuaiji from 347, the year he received his highest title, General of the Right Army. He disliked war and never took part in fighting.</p><p>On 22 April 353 he hosted 41 friends, relatives and pupils at the Orchid Pavilion on Mount Kuaiji for the Double Third Festival. They played a winding stream party, composing poems as cups of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> wine floated towards them, and wrote 41 poems. Wang composed the Lantingji xu as a preface to the collection. In 355 he resigned, citing ill health, after a rival, Wang Shu, gained oversight of Kuaiji and investigated its finances. He moved to Jinting, devoted himself to Taoist practice, and died around 361.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Wang was skilled in regular, semi-cursive and cursive script, and is best known for his semi-cursive work, the style of the Lantingji xu. Apart from that preface and his letters, he mainly produced copies of existing texts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Emperor Taizong of Tang</a> gathered over 2,000 of his works, had court scholars study his technique, and had tracing copies and ink rubbings made, which set Wang and his style as the standard of Chinese calligraphy. No original work by him is known to survive, only rubbings and a few tracing copies.</p><p>The authenticity of the Lantingji xu has been debated since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>, and in 1965 Guo Moruo rejected Wang's authorship. The discovery of an Eastern Jin tomb inscribed in regular script in 1988 weakened one of his arguments. Wang was named the Sage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">Calligraphy</a> around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>, and his style was passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-period">Heian periods</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wang Xizhi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Wang Xizhi</b>, whose preface to the poems composed at the Orchid Pavilion gathering of 353 became the most celebrated work of Chinese calligraphy, was revered as the 'sage of calligraphy'; the painter Gu Kaizhi, to whom the Admonitions of the Instructress to the Court Ladies scroll is attributed,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It deeply influenced Chinese poetry, from Tao Yuanming to Li Bai, landscape painting, with its vast mountains and tiny human figures, and calligraphy; the great calligrapher <b>Wang Xizhi</b> came from a family of Celestial Master followers.</blockquote><a href="/religion/daoism">Daoism · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Linyi, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.1,118.35&amp;year=340" aria-label="Linyi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Wang Xizhi"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4885 4563h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4885 4563h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.1,118.35&amp;year=340">What was here in 340 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
